WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.619
Your first three papers all fit the query

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:06.273
well, but cover the same aspect using

00:00:06.293 --> 00:00:07.549
similar evidence.

00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:10.320
A lower ranked paper covers another useful

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.498
aspect. Which one would you want next when

00:00:12.518 --> 00:00:13.837
exploring the topic?

00:00:13.987 --> 00:00:15.113
Pause and predict.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:19.525
Result diversification asks what a

00:00:19.545 --> 00:00:22.153
candidate adds to the items already

00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:24.898
selected. It changes the objective from

00:00:24.918 --> 00:00:27.888
sorting independent scores to constructing

00:00:27.908 --> 00:00:30.079
a useful set. The answer depends on the

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:32.592
search task and what the system counts as

00:00:32.612 --> 00:00:33.338
different.

00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:36.619
The chapter’s schematic pool concerns open

00:00:36.639 --> 00:00:38.926
access citation advantage.

00:00:39.088 --> 00:00:42.456
A, B and C cover overall advantage.

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:45.085
D covers disciplinary differences.

00:00:45.248 --> 00:00:47.753
E covers selection bias methods.

00:00:47.902 --> 00:00:49.802
F is weakly related.

00:00:50.384 --> 00:00:55.070
One selection is A, B, C: three strong

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.234
individual matches with repeated aspect

00:00:57.254 --> 00:01:02.028
coverage. Another is A, D, E: retain a

00:01:02.048 --> 00:01:04.657
strong match and expose two other useful

00:01:04.677 --> 00:01:07.750
aspects. F stays out because being

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.566
different is insufficient.

00:01:09.742 --> 00:01:12.093
These stipulated selections illustrate an

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:14.541
objective, not a calculated algorithm

00:01:14.561 --> 00:01:15.327
output.

00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:18.708
Diversification names an objective.

00:01:18.857 --> 00:01:21.904
Reranking names a pipeline position.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:24.636
Deduplication removes records considered

00:01:24.656 --> 00:01:26.736
equivalent under a separate rule.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.675
B and C remain distinct studies even

00:01:29.695 --> 00:01:32.278
though they share an aspect with A; shared

00:01:32.298 --> 00:01:34.211
subject matter does not make them

00:01:34.231 --> 00:01:35.229
duplicates.

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:37.987
Difference might mean topic, method,

00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:41.015
geography, viewpoint or merely wording.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:42.975
Broader topical coverage does not

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:45.745
automatically create viewpoint diversity,

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:48.154
fairness or balanced evidence.

00:01:48.317 --> 00:01:50.449
The system’s representation of difference

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:52.382
needs to match what the reader actually

00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:53.670
wants to explore.

00:01:54.578 --> 00:01:57.367
Exploratory search may benefit from less

00:01:57.387 --> 00:02:00.704
repetition. A systematic review may need

00:02:00.724 --> 00:02:03.359
every relevant study, including very

00:02:03.379 --> 00:02:06.155
similar ones. Diversity aware selection

00:02:06.175 --> 00:02:08.707
can push a highly relevant near neighbour

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:11.464
below the displayed cut off.

00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:14.222
Similar papers can still contain separate

00:02:14.242 --> 00:02:15.124
evidence.

00:02:15.687 --> 00:02:18.752
Diversification does not retrieve records

00:02:18.772 --> 00:02:20.143
missing from the pool.

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.714
Evaluate retrieval, diversity and display

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:25.350
limits separately.

00:02:25.499 --> 00:02:27.902
A broader looking list can be useful

00:02:27.922 --> 00:02:30.247
without being an exhaustive account of the

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:31.187
evidence.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:34.332
The chapter’s published PubMed Best Match

00:02:34.352 --> 00:02:37.283
account starts with BM twenty five and

00:02:37.303 --> 00:02:39.757
applies LambdaMART to the top five hundred

00:02:39.777 --> 00:02:42.540
candidates. Its list continues below that

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.362
reranked region in Best Match order.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.706
Primo Research Assistant has another

00:02:47.726 --> 00:02:50.322
boundary: up to thirty candidates are

00:02:50.342 --> 00:02:52.976
reranked using embeddings, and five

00:02:52.996 --> 00:02:55.321
selected abstracts reach generation.

00:02:55.496 --> 00:02:57.886
These are documentation accounts, not new

00:02:57.906 --> 00:03:00.528
service tests. Neither count measures

00:03:00.548 --> 00:03:03.028
relevance, and the two products handle

00:03:03.048 --> 00:03:04.548
their tails differently.

00:03:05.447 --> 00:03:07.823
The documented Primo workflow starts when

00:03:07.843 --> 00:03:10.825
an LLM converts a question into Boolean

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:13.402
variations connected with OR.

00:03:13.564 --> 00:03:16.495
Some filters can be refined automatically.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.201
CDI then retrieves through a largely

00:03:19.221 --> 00:03:21.572
lexical inverted index route.

00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:24.784
Its ranking combines lexical and field

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:27.889
evidence, phrase and proximity signals,

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.609
and static signals such as recency and

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:33.856
citations. The embedding reranking stage

00:03:33.876 --> 00:03:37.077
receives up to thirty leading candidates.

00:03:37.227 --> 00:03:39.410
An AI component later in the chain does

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.502
not make the first retrieval stage dense.

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:45.806
The documented working set for generation

00:03:45.852 --> 00:03:47.610
is five abstracts.

00:03:47.772 --> 00:03:49.595
A paper omitted before the thirty

00:03:49.615 --> 00:03:52.610
candidate reranking boundary cannot be

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:53.692
reordered there.

00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:56.141
A candidate outside the five source

00:03:56.161 --> 00:03:59.015
boundary is also unavailable to that

00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:01.398
answer writer’s documented working set.

00:04:01.955 --> 00:04:04.830
And an abstract is not the complete paper.

00:04:04.979 --> 00:04:07.859
A detail stated only in full text was not

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:10.243
supplied in this documented abstract based

00:04:10.263 --> 00:04:11.183
context.

00:04:12.088 --> 00:04:15.170
Learning to rank, including LambdaMART,

00:04:15.384 --> 00:04:17.581
learns to combine available features from

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:18.946
training examples.

00:04:19.121 --> 00:04:21.254
Those features can be lexical matches,

00:04:21.274 --> 00:04:24.643
dates, publication types or usage; they

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:26.214
need not be embeddings.

00:04:26.377 --> 00:04:29.114
Its limits include the evidence and labels

00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:30.235
it receives.

00:04:30.787 --> 00:04:33.449
Ask which route supplied candidates, how

00:04:33.469 --> 00:04:36.310
many received each comparison, what text

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.539
survived, and whether the selected set

00:04:38.559 --> 00:04:39.789
serves the task.

00:04:39.951 --> 00:04:42.946
Chapter Ten adds another decision:

00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:45.537
combining or routing multiple retrieval

00:04:45.557 --> 00:04:48.488
routes whose ranked lists and score scales

00:04:48.508 --> 00:04:49.441
may differ.
